<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yasna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yasna liturgy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yasna is the Avestan name both of a text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> and of the Yasna liturgy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>'s principal act of worship. The ceremony is meant to strengthen the ordered creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> against the destructive forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahriman">Angra Mainyu</a>, and culminates in the offering to the waters. A trained priest recites it in about two hours, in the morning. The text has 72 chapters composed at different times, including the oldest of the canon: the four most sacred prayers and the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a>, which tradition holds were composed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> himself.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around them stand later texts in Young Avestan: the <b>Yasna</b>, the liturgy of the main act of worship, which contains the Gathas; the Yashts, hymns to individual divine beings; the Vendidad, a book of laws of purity; and prayers for daily use.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Yasna</b>, from a word meaning worship or oblations, is recited in the main Zoroastrian liturgy and has 72 sections called ha.</blockquote><a href="/work/avesta">Avesta · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
